FORMAT: 1A
HOST: http://localhost:8081

# Kontor Storage Node API

REST API for the Kontor Storage Node, a storage node for the Kontor distributed storage network.

This API allows clients to retrieve node information and access stored files.

## Group Node

Resources related to the storage node itself.

### Node Information [GET /api/info]

Retrieve information about the storage node.

+ Response 200 (application/json)

    + Attributes
        + node_id: `abc123def456` (string, required) - Unique identifier of the storage node, `null` until the Portal has registered it
        + phase: `running` (string, required) - Where the node is in its life: `uninitialized`, `sealed`, `activating`, `unregistered`, `running` or `failed`
        + serving: `true` (boolean, required) - Whether the node is serving files and proving storage. Only `true` in the `running` phase; this is the field to alert on, since the server answers this request in every phase

    + Body

            {
                "node_id": "abc123def456",
                "phase": "running",
                "serving": true
            }

## Group Files

Resources related to file storage and retrieval.

### List Files [GET /files{?offset,limit}]

Retrieve a paginated list of available files on this node. Only files that have been fully downloaded are included.

+ Parameters
    + offset: `0` (number, optional) - Number of files to skip
        + Default: `0`
    + limit: `20` (number, optional) - Maximum number of files to return (max: 100)
        + Default: `20`

+ Response 200 (application/json)

    + Attributes
        + offset: 0 (number, required) - Current offset
        + limit: 20 (number, required) - Applied limit
        + total: 42 (number, required) - Total number of available files
        + files (array[FileEntry], required) - List of file entries

    + Body

            {
                "offset": 0,
                "limit": 20,
                "total": 42,
                "files": [
                    {
                        "file_id": "QmXoypizjW3WknFiJnKLwHCnL72vedxjQkDDP1mXWo6uco",
                        "filename": "document.pdf",
                        "mime_type": "application/pdf",
                        "original_size": 1048576,
                        "status": "active",
                        "downloaded": true,
                        "created_at": "2025-01-15T10:30:00Z",
                        "download_path": "/files/QmXoypizjW3WknFiJnKLwHCnL72vedxjQkDDP1mXWo6uco"
                    }
                ]
            }

+ Response 400 (text/plain)

    Returned when the request parameters are invalid (e.g., limit is 0).

    + Body

            limit must be greater than 0

+ Response 500 (text/plain)

    Returned when an internal server error occurs.

    + Body

            internal server error

### Download File [GET /files/{file_id}]

Download a specific file by its ID.

For local storage, the file content is streamed directly. For GCS storage, a temporary redirect to a signed URL is returned.

+ Parameters
    + file_id: `QmXoypizjW3WknFiJnKLwHCnL72vedxjQkDDP1mXWo6uco` (string, required) - Unique identifier of the file

+ Response 200

    File content is streamed with appropriate headers.

    + Headers

            Content-Type: application/pdf
            Content-Disposition: attachment; filename="document.pdf"
            Content-Length: 1048576
            Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable
            Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *
            Access-Control-Allow-Methods: GET, HEAD, OPTIONS

    + Body

            (binary file content)

+ Response 307

    Returned when the file is stored on GCS. The client should follow the redirect to download the file.

    + Headers

            Location: https://storage.googleapis.com/bucket/file?signature=...

+ Response 404 (text/plain)

    Returned when the file does not exist.

    + Body

            file not found

+ Response 409 (text/plain)

    Returned when the file exists but is not yet available (still being downloaded).

    + Body

            file is not yet available on this node

+ Response 502 (text/plain)

    Returned when there is an error communicating with the upstream storage (GCS).

    + Body

            gcs signed url failed: ...

+ Response 503 (text/plain)

    Returned when the storage backend is unavailable.

    + Body

            storage backend unavailable

+ Response 500 (text/plain)

    Returned when an internal server error occurs.

    + Body

            (error details)

## Group Admin

The operator UI at `/admin` and the endpoints behind it.

These are disabled unless `ADMIN_PASSWORD` is configured; without it, login answers `503 ADMIN_UI_DISABLED`. The password is exchanged for a session token that expires after 8 hours and is signed with a key generated at startup, so restarting the node invalidates every session. Errors are returned as `{"error": {"code": ..., "message": ...}}`, where `message` is safe to display — internal causes are logged by the node, never returned.

Unlike the file endpoints, these carry no CORS headers: they are only ever called same-origin by the embedded page.

### Operator UI [GET /admin]

The single-page operator UI. Public — it is the login screen — and contains no secrets. Served with `Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'`, `X-Frame-Options: DENY` and `Cache-Control: no-store`. `GET /admin/` resolves the same page.

+ Response 200 (text/html; charset=utf-8)

### Sign In [POST /api/admin/login]

Exchange the configured password for a session token.

+ Request (application/json)

    + Attributes
        + password: `correct horse battery staple` (string, required) - Value of `ADMIN_PASSWORD`

+ Response 200 (application/json)

    + Body

            {
                "token": "eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiJ9...",
                "expires_at": 1798761600,
                "expires_in": 28800
            }

+ Response 401 (application/json)

    Wrong password (`INVALID_PASSWORD`). Every rejection costs a fixed 250 ms.

+ Response 429 (application/json)

    More than 10 failed attempts from this client in 15 minutes (`TOO_MANY_ATTEMPTS`). The correct password is refused too until the window expires.

+ Response 503 (application/json)

    `ADMIN_PASSWORD` is not configured (`ADMIN_UI_DISABLED`).

### Node Status [GET /api/admin/status]

Counts of everything a reset would erase, plus where it is stored.

+ Request

    + Headers

            Authorization: Bearer eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiJ9...

+ Response 200 (application/json)

    + Body

            {
                "node_id": "abc123def456",
                "registration": {
                    "supported": true,
                    "registered": true,
                    "bls_pubkey": "92538f03...",
                    "node_id": "abc123def456",
                    "signer_id": 42,
                    "derived": {
                        "account": 2,
                        "network": "signet",
                        "taproot_address": "tb1p...",
                        "node_id": "node-8f3a..."
                    }
                },
                "storage": {
                    "method": "local",
                    "directory": "/var/lib/kontor/files"
                },
                "agreements": {
                    "total": 42,
                    "downloaded": 40,
                    "stored_bytes": 1048576
                },
                "challenges": {
                    "total": 7
                },
                "ledger": {
                    "placed": 38,
                    "awaiting_ledger_index": 2,
                    "highest_slot": 613,
                    "rebuild_blocked": false
                },
                "restart_available": true,
                "session_expires_at": 1798761600
            }

    `storage.directory` is `null` when the backend is GCS. `restart_available` is whether the node can be asked to restart itself after a reset — false when neither `BLS_PASSWORD` nor `KONTOR_STORAGE_MNEMONIC` is set, because a restarted process would stop at the key password prompt.

    `registration` says whether the Portal knows this node's BLS key. `registered: false` means onboarding stopped at the lookup and no daemon is running — the node serves this page and nothing else until the key is registered. `node_id` and `signer_id` are then `null`, and `signer_id` is also `null` on a node whose `RegisterBlsKey` transaction has not confirmed yet. `supported: false` means no BLS key was loaded at all, so there is nothing to register.

    `registration.derived` is present only on a node running from `KONTOR_STORAGE_MNEMONIC`, and is `null` otherwise. Such a node registers itself during onboarding and has no use for the wallet flow, so what the page shows instead is the account it derived and the Taproot address a Portal admin has to whitelist. `derived.account` is the wallet's own account number — the value of `KONTOR_STORAGE_ACCOUNT_INDEX`, counting from 1, not the child index in the derivation path. `derived.network` is one of `mainnet`, `testnet`, `signet`, `regtest`, and decides the address prefix as well as the coin type. Its `node_id` is the identifier derived from the xpub, which equals the outer `node_id` once the Portal has the row. While `registered` is `false` such a node re-offers its registration every 30 seconds, so `derived.taproot_address` being whitelisted is the only action left — this endpoint starts reporting `registered: true` on its own once it is. The exception is a registration the Portal read and rejected outright (`400`, `401`, `409`, `422`): replaying identical bytes cannot change that answer, so the node logs it and stops, and `registered` then only turns `true` if somebody registers the key elsewhere.

    `ledger` reports whether the node can still prove. `placed` is how many stored files carry a contract-assigned slot and would enter the rebuilt ledger; `awaiting_ledger_index` is how many are stored and served but not yet provable, because the Portal has not reconciled their creating transaction. `rebuild_blocked` is `true` once `highest_slot + 1` exceeds `placed` by more than kontor-crypto's sparsity gap (1024), at which point the ledger cannot be rebuilt and every proof is skipped — see the README.

+ Response 401 (application/json)

    Missing, malformed or expired session (`UNAUTHORIZED`).

+ Response 503 (application/json)

    `ADMIN_PASSWORD` is not configured (`ADMIN_UI_DISABLED`).

### Reset Database [POST /api/admin/reset-database]

Deletes every row of `agreements` and `challenges` in a single transaction. Both tables are chain-derived, so after a Kontor signet reset they describe a chain that no longer exists; the daemon re-fetches agreements from the Portal afterwards.

With `delete_files`, the stored blobs of the deleted rows are removed too — local files, or objects in the node's GCS bucket. Only blobs named by those rows are touched, so the BLS key and everything else in the storage directory survive. Blob deletion never fails the request: the rows are already gone, and the counts say what happened.

With `restart`, the node answers this request and then exits with code `75` so its supervisor starts it again. The wipe alone leaves the node's Portal registration in place — it was never in the database — but the `signer_id`, JWT and announced URL read during onboarding are stale after a signet reset, and only a fresh start replaces them. If the Portal no longer knows the node's BLS key, the restarted process stops at the registration card rather than exiting; see `POST /api/admin/register-node`. Refused with `RESTART_UNAVAILABLE` when the node could not come back unattended; check `restart_available` on `GET /api/admin/status` first.

+ Request (application/json)

    + Headers

            Authorization: Bearer eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiJ9...

    + Attributes
        + confirm: `RESET` (string, required) - Must be exactly `RESET`
        + delete_files: false (boolean, optional) - Also delete the stored file blobs
            + Default: `false`
        + restart: false (boolean, optional) - Exit after the wipe so the supervisor restarts the node
            + Default: `false`

+ Response 200 (application/json)

    + Body

            {
                "deleted": {
                    "challenges": 7,
                    "agreements": 42
                },
                "total_rows_deleted": 49,
                "storage_method": "local",
                "files": {
                    "requested": 40,
                    "deleted": 39,
                    "missing": 1,
                    "failed": 0,
                    "skipped": 0
                },
                "restarting": false
            }

    `files` is `null` when `delete_files` was not set. `missing` covers a local file that had already disappeared or a bucket object GCS answers `404` for — nothing to act on. `failed` is a blob still out there: the row is gone, so only the operator can deal with it now. `skipped` counts file ids that were not plain object names and were therefore never turned into a path.

    `restarting` echoes whether the node is on its way down. The response is flushed before the process exits, so this is the last thing the node says.

+ Response 400 (application/json)

    `confirm` was not `RESET` (`CONFIRMATION_REQUIRED`), or `restart` was asked for on a node with no `BLS_PASSWORD` in its environment (`RESTART_UNAVAILABLE`). Nothing is deleted in either case.

+ Response 401 (application/json)

    Missing, malformed or expired session (`UNAUTHORIZED`).

+ Response 500 (application/json)

    The transaction could not be opened or committed (`DATABASE_ERROR`). The wipe is rolled back.

### Register Node [POST /api/admin/register-node]

Registers this node's BLS key with the Portal, so a node whose key the Portal has forgotten — after a Portal database reset, say — can be brought back without leaving the node's own UI.

The body carries only what the node cannot produce for itself: the extended public key and the Schnorr proof of possession, both from Horizon Wallet's `getBLSPoP`, plus the BLS key the wallet reports. The node derives the x-only pubkey from `xpubkey`, signs the BLS binding and the `RegisterBlsKey` op with its own key, and posts all five fields to the Portal's `POST /api/nodes/register`.

`bls_pubkey` must be this node's own key. Registering any other would write a Portal row the node cannot sign for, leaving the same failed lookup one restart later, so a mismatch is refused before the Portal is called at all.

On success the node asks to restart, since onboarding is what turns a registration into a running node — including waiting for the `RegisterBlsKey` transaction to confirm on-chain. A node with no `BLS_PASSWORD` cannot restart unattended, so it answers `restarting: false` and stays up; restart it yourself once the Portal has confirmed the key.

+ Request (application/json)

    + Headers

            Authorization: Bearer eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiJ9...

    + Attributes
        + xpubkey: `tpubDD5...` (string, required) - Extended public key from `getBLSPoP`
        + bls_pubkey: `92538f03...` (string, required) - 192 hex characters; must be this node's key
        + schnorr_sig: `4a1c...` (string, required) - 128 hex characters, from `getBLSPoP`

+ Response 200 (application/json)

    + Body

            {
                "node_id": "abc123def456",
                "bls_pubkey": "92538f03...",
                "restarting": true
            }

+ Response 400 (application/json)

    A field was malformed, or `bls_pubkey` is not this node's key (`INVALID_REGISTRATION`). Nothing was sent to the Portal.

+ Response 401 (application/json)

    Missing, malformed or expired session (`UNAUTHORIZED`).

+ Response 409 (application/json)

    No BLS key is loaded, so there is nothing to register (`REGISTRATION_UNAVAILABLE`).

+ Response 502 (application/json)

    The Portal refused the registration or could not be reached (`PORTAL_ERROR`). Its own message is forwarded — most usefully `Bitcoin address … is not whitelisted for node registration`, which only a Portal admin can clear.

### Sealed Envelope [GET /api/admin/envelope]

Mints a one-shot channel between the operator's browser and this process, for the three secrets that must never appear in a body a proxy might log: the seed phrase at setup, a seal passphrase, and the seal key handed back when an env slot is added.

The browser mints its own ephemeral P-256 keypair, does ECDH against `node_pubkey`, and runs the shared secret through HKDF-SHA256 — salted with `session_id`, `info` = `KONTOR_ADMIN_ENVELOPE_V1` — expanded to **64 bytes**. Those are two AES-256-GCM keys: the first seals browser to node, the second node to browser, so a reply can never be replayed as a request. Every seal carries `session_id` as additional authenticated data, which binds a ciphertext to the one envelope it was made for.

The envelope is spent by the first request that presents it, successful or not, and a second `GET` replaces any envelope still outstanding.

This defends against **passive capture** — an edge that logs bodies, an APM agent that samples them — and not against an attacker who controls the response path, who could hand out an envelope of their own. The out-of-band check is `fingerprint`: the node logs it when the envelope is issued and `/admin` shows it, so the two can be compared.

+ Request

    + Headers

            Authorization: Bearer eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiJ9...

+ Response 200 (application/json)

    + Attributes
        + session_id: `9f86d081884c7d659a2feaa0c55ad015` (string, required) - 16 random bytes, hex. HKDF salt and AEAD additional authenticated data
        + node_pubkey: `04a1b2...` (string, required) - The node's ephemeral P-256 public key, uncompressed SEC1, hex — the encoding WebCrypto's `"raw"` export produces
        + fingerprint: `1fac2362` (string, required) - Short identifier for that key, also logged by the node
        + expires_in: 300 (number, required) - Seconds until the envelope stops working

+ Response 401 (application/json)

    Missing, malformed or expired session (`UNAUTHORIZED`).

### Provision Node [POST /api/admin/setup/provision]

Gives a node with no key its identity. Available only in the `uninitialized` phase — a node that is merely `sealed` is refused too, because provisioning over a keystore nobody can currently open would replace an identity that is only temporarily out of reach.

The sealed payload is `{"mnemonic": ..., "account": ..., "network": ...}`. `account` is numbered as Horizon Wallet numbers it, from 1; `0` is refused by name rather than read as the first account. The phrase lives in the process only as long as it takes to derive a key and is never written anywhere: what persists is the sealed BLS key and the public identity.

On success the node enters the `activating` phase before answering, so a second request cannot provision a different identity over the first.

+ Request (application/json)

    + Headers

            Authorization: Bearer eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiJ9...

    + Attributes
        + session_id: `9f86d081884c7d659a2feaa0c55ad015` (string, required) - The envelope this body was sealed to
        + client_pubkey: `04c3d4...` (string, required) - The browser's ephemeral P-256 public key, uncompressed SEC1, hex
        + nonce: `1a2b3c4d5e6f70819a2b3c4d` (string, required) - 12-byte AES-GCM nonce, hex
        + ciphertext: `8f2c...` (string, required) - The sealed payload, hex

+ Response 200 (application/json)

    + Body

            {
                "node_id": "node-deac9e85...",
                "account": 2,
                "network": "signet",
                "taproot_address": "tb1p...",
                "bls_pubkey": "a1b2...",
                "seal_slot": "file",
                "seal_fingerprint": "2bd8450f"
            }

    `taproot_address` is what a Portal admin has to whitelist before the node can register.

+ Response 400 (application/json)

    The envelope is unknown, expired or its payload did not authenticate (`INVALID_ENVELOPE`), or what it contained is not a usable seed phrase (`INVALID_SETUP`). Nothing is written in either case.

+ Response 401 (application/json)

    Missing, malformed or expired session (`UNAUTHORIZED`).

+ Response 409 (application/json)

    This node already has a key (`SETUP_UNAVAILABLE`).

### Seal Status [GET /api/admin/seal]

How this node's key is sealed right now. Nothing here is secret.

`opened_with` is recorded rather than inferred, and the distinction is load-bearing: "there is an env slot" is not the same claim as "this boot came up on it", and only the second makes dropping the file slot safe.

+ Request

    + Headers

            Authorization: Bearer eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiJ9...

+ Response 200 (application/json)

    + Body

            {
                "slots": ["file", "env"],
                "opened_with": "file",
                "fingerprint": "2bd8450f",
                "can_add_env_slot": false,
                "can_drop_file_slot": false,
                "env_var": "KONTOR_SEAL_KEY"
            }

+ Response 401 (application/json)

    Missing, malformed or expired session (`UNAUTHORIZED`).

### Add Env Slot [POST /api/admin/seal/env-slot]

Generates a key for `KONTOR_SEAL_KEY` and adds a slot for it. The file slot is untouched, so the node still starts exactly as before; this is step one of a two-step hardening flow with a redeploy in between.

The key is returned **once**, sealed to the browser that asked, and the node keeps no copy it can read back. Together with the volume it *is* this node's BLS key, which is why it does not travel in the clear. If it is lost before being pasted in, call this again: the previous env slot is replaced.

The request carries an envelope even though it sends nothing secret up. Opening it first is what proves the browser derived the same keys before the node commits a slot to them.

+ Request (application/json)

    + Headers

            Authorization: Bearer eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiJ9...

    + Attributes
        + session_id: `9f86d081884c7d659a2feaa0c55ad015` (string, required) - The envelope this reply is sealed to
        + client_pubkey: `04c3d4...` (string, required) - The browser's ephemeral P-256 public key, hex
        + nonce: `1a2b3c4d5e6f70819a2b3c4d` (string, required) - 12-byte AES-GCM nonce, hex
        + ciphertext: `8f2c...` (string, required) - A sealed empty object, hex

+ Response 200 (application/json)

    + Body

            {
                "env_var": "KONTOR_SEAL_KEY",
                "fingerprint": "7c1e9a04",
                "sealed": {
                    "nonce": "9b8a7c6d5e4f30211a2b3c4d",
                    "ciphertext": "d41f..."
                },
                "next": "Set KONTOR_SEAL_KEY=<key> on this deployment and restart. ..."
            }

    `sealed` opens under the node-to-browser key to `{"env_var": ..., "key": ...}`, where `key` is 32 random bytes, base64. `fingerprint` is outside the envelope on purpose: it reveals nothing and is what the operator compares against the node's logs after the redeploy.

+ Response 400 (application/json)

    The envelope is unknown, expired or its payload did not authenticate (`INVALID_ENVELOPE`). No slot is written.

+ Response 401 (application/json)

    Missing, malformed or expired session (`UNAUTHORIZED`).

+ Response 409 (application/json)

    There is no open keystore to add a slot to (`SEAL_UNAVAILABLE`).

### Drop File Slot [POST /api/admin/seal/drop-file-slot]

Removes the `file` slot and deletes `.seal-key`, leaving the node openable only from its environment. Step three of the hardening flow, and refused until the node has actually started through the env slot — check `can_drop_file_slot` on `GET /api/admin/seal` first.

+ Request (application/json)

    + Headers

            Authorization: Bearer eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiJ9...

    + Attributes
        + confirm: `DROP` (string, required) - Must be exactly `DROP`

+ Response 200 (application/json)

    + Body

            {
                "slots": ["env"]
            }

+ Response 400 (application/json)

    `confirm` was not `DROP` (`CONFIRMATION_REQUIRED`). Nothing is removed.

+ Response 401 (application/json)

    Missing, malformed or expired session (`UNAUTHORIZED`).

+ Response 409 (application/json)

    This boot did not come up through the env slot, so removing the file slot could leave the node unopenable (`SEAL_UNAVAILABLE`).

### Add Passphrase Slot [POST /api/admin/seal/passphrase]

Adds a slot only a human can open, from a sealed `{"passphrase": ...}` payload. Added *alongside* the machine-supplied slots, never instead of them, so unattended restarts keep working — the node refuses to be left with nothing a machine can supply.

This is the answer to a lost seal key. Without such a slot, a node that loses both its `.seal-key` and its `KONTOR_SEAL_KEY` starts in the `sealed` phase and stays there.

The floor is 20 characters, higher than the admin password's and for a different reason: this one is not behind a login throttle, so anyone holding the keystore file can try it offline as fast as their hardware allows.

+ Request (application/json)

    + Headers

            Authorization: Bearer eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiJ9...

    + Attributes
        + session_id: `9f86d081884c7d659a2feaa0c55ad015` (string, required) - The envelope this body was sealed to
        + client_pubkey: `04c3d4...` (string, required) - The browser's ephemeral P-256 public key, hex
        + nonce: `1a2b3c4d5e6f70819a2b3c4d` (string, required) - 12-byte AES-GCM nonce, hex
        + ciphertext: `8f2c...` (string, required) - The sealed payload, hex

+ Response 200 (application/json)

    + Body

            {
                "slots": ["file", "passphrase"]
            }

+ Response 400 (application/json)

    The envelope did not authenticate (`INVALID_ENVELOPE`), or the passphrase is shorter than 20 characters (`WEAK_PASSWORD`). The floor is checked on the decrypted payload, so a short one is named rather than sealed away into a slot.

+ Response 401 (application/json)

    Missing, malformed or expired session (`UNAUTHORIZED`).

+ Response 409 (application/json)

    There is no open keystore (`SEAL_UNAVAILABLE`).

### Unseal Node [POST /api/admin/unseal]

Opens a node in the `sealed` phase with a sealed `{"passphrase": ...}` payload. Without this, that phase is a dead end: the key is on the volume and nothing on the machine can open it.

The passphrase is checked before the supervisor is asked to do anything, so a wrong one is an error the operator sees rather than a phase change that fails somewhere they are not looking. The node does not go on to report that it could restart unattended — nothing on this machine supplied the key.

+ Request (application/json)

    + Headers

            Authorization: Bearer eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiJ9...

    + Attributes
        + session_id: `9f86d081884c7d659a2feaa0c55ad015` (string, required) - The envelope this body was sealed to
        + client_pubkey: `04c3d4...` (string, required) - The browser's ephemeral P-256 public key, hex
        + nonce: `1a2b3c4d5e6f70819a2b3c4d` (string, required) - 12-byte AES-GCM nonce, hex
        + ciphertext: `8f2c...` (string, required) - The sealed payload, hex

+ Response 200 (application/json)

    + Body

            {
                "unsealing": true
            }

    The node moves out of `sealed` on the supervisor's next tick; poll `GET /api/info` for the phase.

+ Response 400 (application/json)

    The envelope did not authenticate (`INVALID_ENVELOPE`).

+ Response 401 (application/json)

    That passphrase does not open this node's keystore (`INVALID_PASSWORD`).

+ Response 409 (application/json)

    This node is not sealed, or has no keystore to unseal (`SEAL_UNAVAILABLE`).

## Data Structures

### FileEntry (object)

+ file_id: `QmXoypizjW3WknFiJnKLwHCnL72vedxjQkDDP1mXWo6uco` (string, required) - Unique identifier of the file (content-addressed hash)
+ filename: `document.pdf` (string, required) - Original filename
+ mime_type: `application/pdf` (string, required) - MIME type of the file
+ original_size: 1048576 (number, optional) - Size of the file in bytes
+ status: `active` (string, required) - Status of the agreement
+ downloaded: true (boolean, required) - Whether the file has been fully downloaded
+ created_at: `2025-01-15T10:30:00Z` (string, required) - ISO 8601 timestamp of when the agreement was created
